The ratio of the altitude ($BD$) to the hypotenuse ($AC$) is $x/(2x\sqrt{3})=x/(2x)\sqrt{3}=\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{3}$.

This quantity equals $\frac{3}{AC}$, so $AC=6\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}$, so $AC=2\sqrt{3}$.

Thus $x=BD/4=3/4$, so the base is $x=3/4$ and the height is $x\sqrt{3}=3\sqrt{3}/4$.  So the area is $\left(\frac{3}{4}\right)\left(\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{4}\right)=\boxed{\frac{9\sqrt{3}}{16}}$ square units. </think> <answer> \frac{9\sqrt{3}}{16} </answer>

 Since we may add or remove sides to a convex polygon and not change the number of right angles, adding the minimum number of sides necessary to form a convex decagon, it maximizes the number of right angles, we can assume we have a convex decagon.

A convex decagon has 7 interior angles less than 180 degrees, due to the remaining 3 less than 180. We are told that we want to maximize the number of right angles. So we have to put a right angle on each of the angles less than 180. The corresponding angle above is congruent, therefore one of those angles also has a right angle.

If we put a right angle somewhere in each of the angles less than 180, there will be three angles of 135 degrees (which, must be the remaining 3 angles of the decagon). We still have 4 angles to fill in Now, since a convex angle can't have more than 7 degrees, the remaining angles can have at most 1 degree. This results in at most one angle of 45 degrees and one angle of 90 degrees.

We can also have two angles of 90 degrees by putting a right angle somewhere in each of the four interior right angles. By doing so, we have a total of $\boxed{6}$ right angles.

